1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,780
Picture an AI agent with access to refund system,

2
00:00:04,820 --> 00:00:06,300
the email tool,

3
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:07,720
and the customer database.

4
00:00:08,360 --> 00:00:10,020
Now picture its requirements.

5
00:00:10,340 --> 00:00:11,180
One line.

6
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:13,080
Handle refunds.

7
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:15,000
That's the whole job description.

8
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:18,660
We wouldn't give a new hire a company card

9
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:19,140
with that.

10
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:22,900
Somehow we do it to software that never sleeps.

11
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:25,560
And it's already biting!

12
00:00:26,140 --> 00:00:29,040
In an October survey by Redwood Software,

13
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:34,340
77% of senior tech and risk leaders said ungoverned

14
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:36,940
AI agents had caused problems at their company.

15
00:00:37,380 --> 00:00:40,340
The most common one wasn't a robot uprising,

16
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:43,320
it was an unexpected cloud cost,

17
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:48,020
and only about 58% had a governance policy that

18
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:49,800
actually was enforced.

19
00:00:50,567 --> 00:00:52,027
I'm Prachit,

20
00:00:52,067 --> 00:00:53,267
a business analyst,

21
00:00:53,387 --> 00:00:55,987
and today we write an agent job description.

22
00:00:56,487 --> 00:00:57,727
What it does alone,

23
00:00:57,987 --> 00:00:59,407
what needs a person,

24
00:00:59,747 --> 00:01:01,207
what it must never do,

25
00:01:01,267 --> 00:01:04,667
plus two requirements almost everyone forgets.

26
00:01:05,027 --> 00:01:06,327
One costs money,

27
00:01:06,267 --> 00:01:08,547
and the other one stops everything.

28
00:01:09,233 --> 00:01:11,593
Start with the permission matrix,

29
00:01:11,833 --> 00:01:13,253
Rows are actions,

30
00:01:13,393 --> 00:01:14,373
three columns,

31
00:01:14,553 --> 00:01:15,393
do it alone,

32
00:01:15,713 --> 00:01:16,593
ask first,

33
00:01:16,833 --> 00:01:17,433
never.

34
00:01:17,833 --> 00:01:20,353
For our made-up client Northstar Systems,

35
00:01:20,773 --> 00:01:23,553
the refund agent can read order history alone,

36
00:01:23,793 --> 00:01:25,553
draft a reply alone,

37
00:01:25,973 --> 00:01:29,873
refund under $50 on a single paid order alone,

38
00:01:30,093 --> 00:01:32,333
between $50 and $500,

39
00:01:32,413 --> 00:01:33,733
it asks first,

40
00:01:34,733 --> 00:01:39,573
refund more than the customer paid never change bank

41
00:01:39,273 --> 00:01:44,213
details never email more than one customer at once

42
00:01:45,033 --> 00:01:48,813
Never isn't a polite request.

43
00:01:49,553 --> 00:01:52,413
Write each one like an acceptance criteria.

44
00:01:53,173 --> 00:01:56,673
Given an order that was already refunded,

45
00:01:56,973 --> 00:01:58,653
when a refund is requested,

46
00:01:58,833 --> 00:02:02,273
then the agent doesn't issue it and routes the

47
00:02:01,973 --> 00:02:03,253
case to a person.

48
00:02:04,033 --> 00:02:05,533
Now it's testable,

49
00:02:05,673 --> 00:02:06,713
and in testing,

50
00:02:06,893 --> 00:02:08,733
never means 100%.

51
00:02:08,433 --> 00:02:10,813
Not mostly never.

52
00:02:11,367 --> 00:02:14,027
Careful with ask first though.

53
00:02:14,267 --> 00:02:17,787
If the agent asked a person to approve 200

54
00:02:17,487 --> 00:02:19,407
refunds a day,

55
00:02:19,687 --> 00:02:22,387
by lunchtime nobody is going to read them.

56
00:02:22,847 --> 00:02:24,407
That's not an oversight.

57
00:02:24,787 --> 00:02:27,187
That's a rubber stamp with extra steps.

58
00:02:27,707 --> 00:02:29,867
So write a limit for approvals too.

59
00:02:30,107 --> 00:02:32,967
Few enough that a person actually reads each one.

60
00:02:33,247 --> 00:02:34,607
If the queue's too big,

61
00:02:34,807 --> 00:02:36,027
the threshold is wrong,

62
00:02:36,007 --> 00:02:36,867
not the person.

63
00:02:37,433 --> 00:02:40,293
Now, add a column most matrices get,

64
00:02:40,713 --> 00:02:41,653
blast radius.

65
00:02:41,993 --> 00:02:43,193
How many customers,

66
00:02:43,413 --> 00:02:44,433
how much money,

67
00:02:44,693 --> 00:02:45,933
and can it be undone?

68
00:02:46,273 --> 00:02:48,593
One wrong reply hurts one customer.

69
00:02:48,913 --> 00:02:51,833
One wrong mass email hurts all of them.

70
00:02:52,213 --> 00:02:54,993
Size each permission to the blast radius,

71
00:02:54,993 --> 00:02:57,953
not to how clever the model looked in the

72
00:02:57,653 --> 00:02:58,193
demo.

73
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:00,980
Forgotten requirement number one,

74
00:03:01,160 --> 00:03:01,860
a budget.

75
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:04,620
Max actions per hour,

76
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:06,160
max cost per case,

77
00:03:06,340 --> 00:03:09,160
max retries before it gives up and calls a

78
00:03:08,860 --> 00:03:09,480
person.

79
00:03:10,140 --> 00:03:13,440
An agent stuck in a loop at 3am doesn't

80
00:03:13,140 --> 00:03:13,980
get tired.

81
00:03:14,880 --> 00:03:16,060
It gets expensive.

82
00:03:16,420 --> 00:03:19,580
Remember, cloud costs top that survey.

83
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:22,920
Next, write down when it hands over,

84
00:03:23,020 --> 00:03:24,860
as numbers not adjectives.

85
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:27,400
Low confidence is a vague word.

86
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:30,720
Confidence below 80% is a requirement.

87
00:03:31,240 --> 00:03:32,280
Then the rest,

88
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:34,480
the customer mentions a lawyer.

89
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:36,740
The amount is over the limit,

90
00:03:36,820 --> 00:03:39,500
or it's the same customer for the third time

91
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:39,920
today.

92
00:03:40,420 --> 00:03:42,280
And the handoff carries the context,

93
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:46,580
so that person isn't starting from "see below" with

94
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:47,160
nothing below.

95
00:03:47,767 --> 00:03:50,127
The agent acts as itself,

96
00:03:50,007 --> 00:03:50,947
not as you.

97
00:03:51,267 --> 00:03:52,287
Its own account,

98
00:03:52,307 --> 00:03:53,307
its own permission,

99
00:03:53,407 --> 00:03:54,887
and every action logged,

100
00:03:55,067 --> 00:03:56,027
what it saw,

101
00:03:56,027 --> 00:03:56,927
what it did,

102
00:03:56,827 --> 00:03:57,507
and why.

103
00:03:57,827 --> 00:04:00,327
When audit asked who approved a refund,

104
00:04:00,547 --> 00:04:03,467
the agent probably is not an answer anyone enjoys.

105
00:04:04,167 --> 00:04:06,487
Forgotten requirement number two,

106
00:04:06,607 --> 00:04:07,567
the off switch.

107
00:04:07,907 --> 00:04:09,087
Write it with names,

108
00:04:09,267 --> 00:04:10,527
who can stop it,

109
00:04:10,807 --> 00:04:12,687
what happens to work in flight,

110
00:04:13,127 --> 00:04:14,907
it goes to a person's queue.

111
00:04:16,287 --> 00:04:18,407
And who signs it back on?

112
00:04:18,647 --> 00:04:20,507
If the answer is IT,

113
00:04:20,487 --> 00:04:21,027
I think.

114
00:04:21,627 --> 00:04:23,007
That's not a kill switch.

115
00:04:23,147 --> 00:04:24,107
That's a rumor.

116
00:04:24,667 --> 00:04:28,867
Then test the never rules in UAT and again

117
00:04:28,567 --> 00:04:29,327
in prod.

118
00:04:29,767 --> 00:04:31,167
Plant a canary,

119
00:04:31,507 --> 00:04:32,547
a test order,

120
00:04:32,687 --> 00:04:36,347
in a test account that's already been refunded and

121
00:04:36,047 --> 00:04:39,507
sending a refund request for it every week.

122
00:04:39,727 --> 00:04:41,607
If the agent never pays it,

123
00:04:41,547 --> 00:04:44,287
the alarm goes off before a real customer finds

124
00:04:43,987 --> 00:04:44,647
the gap.

125
00:04:45,167 --> 00:04:46,667
Bonus trick,

126
00:04:46,967 --> 00:04:50,787
give an AI model your permission matrix and ask

127
00:04:50,487 --> 00:04:53,267
it to find a request that sneaks around a

128
00:04:52,967 --> 00:04:53,627
never.

129
00:04:54,147 --> 00:04:58,527
It'll find things like split $900 refund into two

130
00:04:58,227 --> 00:05:00,567
$450 refunds,

131
00:05:00,267 --> 00:05:02,307
both under the approval amount.

132
00:05:02,547 --> 00:05:05,527
Every loophole it finds becomes a new rule.

133
00:05:05,907 --> 00:05:09,347
Here, limits apply to the total per order,

134
00:05:09,247 --> 00:05:10,527
not per transaction.

135
00:05:11,233 --> 00:05:13,493
All of it fits on one page,

136
00:05:13,613 --> 00:05:14,493
the matrix,

137
00:05:15,093 --> 00:05:16,353
the blast radius,

138
00:05:16,553 --> 00:05:17,293
the budget,

139
00:05:17,333 --> 00:05:18,053
the triggers,

140
00:05:18,873 --> 00:05:19,573
the log,

141
00:05:19,653 --> 00:05:20,613
and the off switch.

142
00:05:20,973 --> 00:05:23,773
There's a free agent job description template in my

143
00:05:23,473 --> 00:05:24,553
BA Toolkit,

144
00:05:24,513 --> 00:05:25,653
linked in the description.

145
00:05:26,300 --> 00:05:28,600
And put one name at the top.

146
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:32,960
That survey found responsibility for agents spread across several

147
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:33,380
teams.

148
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:36,040
Spread across teams usually means nobody.

149
00:05:36,520 --> 00:05:38,040
Every hire has a manager,

150
00:05:38,160 --> 00:05:39,440
so does every agent.

151
00:05:39,967 --> 00:05:41,027
So,

152
00:05:41,407 --> 00:05:45,127
a permission matrix with a never column you can

153
00:05:44,827 --> 00:05:48,427
test, sized to the blast radius with a budget,

154
00:05:48,787 --> 00:05:50,107
handover triggers,

155
00:05:50,267 --> 00:05:53,687
a log and an off switch and one owner.

156
00:05:54,233 --> 00:05:55,813
It's quiz time.

157
00:05:56,113 --> 00:05:58,173
Six questions popping up right now.

158
00:05:58,273 --> 00:05:59,333
Easy to hard.

159
00:05:59,553 --> 00:06:00,733
Let's see your score.

160
00:06:35,300 --> 00:06:38,860
Then tell me what's one thing your AI agent

161
00:06:38,560 --> 00:06:42,680
should never do that nobody's written down yet and

162
00:06:42,380 --> 00:06:45,020
if you haven't tested yours from Dev to Prod

163
00:06:44,720 --> 00:06:47,320
that video is linked right here
